The small grid sweep cannot reach the large-star sampling branch because its conservative threshold is at least $128/\epsilon=1280$. A separate unit test therefore uses $N=4096$ and the point $p=(1,N+1)$, whose star has 4096 lines. Exactly 1968 of these keyed rows are negative at $p$, exceeding the required local quarter fraction. On 20 learner seeds, a distribution putting mass $.7$ at $p$ and $.3$ at a positive outside point produced a proper zero-error answer. Full transcripts are saved. This tests the large-star branch at one verified star; it does not claim that the entire $N=4096$ table was generated or tested for regularity.  
